# 21. Interview and Viva Questions

### Q1. What is Prompt Evaluation?

Prompt Evaluation is the process of testing and measuring prompt quality to determine whether an AI model generates accurate, relevant, consistent, and useful responses.

### Q2. Why is Prompt Evaluation required?

It is required to improve response quality, detect errors, measure consistency, and ensure that AI-generated outputs satisfy user requirements.

### Q3. What are the major components of Prompt Evaluation?

The major components include prompts, test datasets, expected outputs, generated outputs, evaluation criteria, metrics, and feedback.

### Q4. What is Manual Evaluation?

Manual Evaluation is a process in which humans examine AI-generated outputs and judge their quality using predefined criteria.

### Q5. What is Automated Evaluation?

Automated Evaluation uses software, code, or mathematical metrics to measure the quality of generated outputs.

### Q6. What is LLM-as-a-Judge?

It is a technique in which one language model evaluates another model's generated output based on predefined evaluation criteria.

### Q7. What is A/B Testing?

A/B Testing compares two prompt versions using the same test cases to identify which prompt performs better.

### Q8. What is Prompt Robustness?

Prompt robustness is the ability of a prompt to produce reliable results when inputs vary or contain unusual conditions.

### Q9. What is the difference between Precision and Recall?

Precision measures how many predicted positive results are actually positive, while Recall measures how many actual positive cases are correctly identified.

### Q10. What is an F1-Score?

F1-score is the harmonic mean of precision and recall. It balances both metrics.

### Q11. What is a Test Dataset?

A test dataset is a collection of inputs used to evaluate the performance of a prompt or AI system.

### Q12. What is Regression Testing in Prompt Evaluation?

Regression testing checks whether changes made to a prompt have negatively affected its previously working behaviour.

### Q13. What is the difference between BLEU and ROUGE?

BLEU is commonly used for translation evaluation and measures n-gram precision with a brevity penalty. ROUGE is commonly used for summarization evaluation and measures overlap with reference text, often emphasizing recall.

### Q14. Can a prompt with high accuracy still be poor?

Yes. A prompt may have high accuracy but still generate unclear, incomplete, irrelevant, or poorly formatted responses.

